==English==

===Etymology===
{{root|en|ine-pro|*dʰeh₁-}}
From {{der|en|fr|déficit}}, from {{der|en|la|dēficit}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ˈdɛfɪsɪt/|a=RP}}
* {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Vealhurl-deficit.wav|a=Southern England}}
* {{IPA|en|/ˈdɛfəsɪt/|a=GA}}
* {{hyphenation|en|de|fi|cit}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# Deficiency in [[amount]] or [[quality]]; a falling [[short]]; [[lack]].
#: {{ux|en|The crop output this year has been comparatively small, owing to the '''deficit''' in rainfall.}}
# A situation wherein, or amount whereby, spending exceeds (e.g. government) revenue.
#* {{quote-journal|en|year=1962|month=October|title=Talking of Trains: Passed to you, Mr. Macmillan|journal=Modern Railways|page=220|text=Dr. Beeching's obvious intent is that if Scottish&mdash;and similarly unprofitable English and Welsh&mdash;railways are to be maintained, it must be done by an unconcealed subsidy; he is determined that the railways shall no longer be preoccupied with&mdash;and derided for&mdash;immense '''deficits''' which include the burden of social services the State must openly underwrite, if it wants them.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|work=Contra Costa Times|title=It's Time for a Reality Check on the Deficit|location=Contra Costa, CA|date=August 4, 1996|passage=But Wall Street, which has a case of '''deficit'''-attention disorder, is no longer focused on a balanced budget. "The bond market only worries about one thing at [a time.]}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=September 28 2013|author=w:Kenan Malik|title=London Is Special, but Not That Special|titleurl=http://www.nytimes.com/2013/09/29/opinion/sunday/london-is-special-but-not-that-special.html?ref=opinion|journal=New York Times|accessdate=28 September 2013
|passage=Economically, too, London is startlingly different. The capital, unlike the country as a whole, has no budget '''deficit''': London’s public spending matches the taxes paid in the city. The average Londoner contributes 70 percent more to Britain’s national income than people in the rest of the country.}}

